Architecture: SwiftUI `View` + `@MainActor @Observable SignalGapDetectorPageModel` driven by two
injected seams (P1/S8) — `SignalGapDetectorVehicleSource` (the fleet feed for the header picker, web
`useVehicles`) and `SignalGapDetectorCatalogProviding` (the bound `SignalCatalogPanelModel` for the
chosen vehicle, web `<SignalCatalogPanel vehicleId>`). No networking/business logic in the view.
The web page is a thin wrapper that reads the global `useSelectedVehicle()` scope and renders either
the "select a vehicle" EmptyState (no scope) or the catalog; the native page reproduces that exactly:
the adaptive header (macOS/iPad regular vs. compact iPhone via horizontalSizeClass, ADR-002/006) with
`TSPageTitle` + subtitle + the shared `VehicleSelect` surface (web `<VehicleSelect/>`), the
always-rendered `TSEmptyState` no-vehicle branch (Activity glyph -> SF Symbol "waveform.path.ecg",
ADR-011 never blank), and the mounted `SignalCatalogPanel` re-keyed on the scope. Shared components
+ design tokens only (TSPageTitle / TSEmptyState / VehicleSelect / SignalCatalogPanel / Color.TS /
Font.TS / TSSpacing). No SI units render on this page (the gap detector is a catalog wrapper; the
embedded catalog handles its own staleness formatting), so no useUnits boundary is needed here.

The composition root binds the production seams over the shared KMP core + live signal store; until
that wiring lands the registration ships sample seams (SampleSignalGapDetectorVehicleSource yields a
2-vehicle fleet but does NOT auto-select, so the page opens on the EmptyState; the catalog provider
binds an in-memory snapshot), the identical staging the sibling LiveSignalInspector page uses.

